  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nFriday, temperatures resume a gradual upward trajectory as the upper \ntrough drifts off to the east.  To the west, an upper trough off the \nwest coast will dig south, pushing weak ridging into West Texas and \nSoutheast New Mexico.  Temperatures will increase through the \nweekend, w/latest model runs maxing out temperatures for this \nforecast Sunday or Columbus Day, when highs will be around 90 most \nlocations, a good 10 F or so above normal.  As the upper trough digs \ndown the west coast and weak ridging moves east, southwest flow \naloft will develop over West Texas and Southeast New Mexico, w/the \ntrough sending shortwaves our way beginning Monday afternoon or so. \nThis will result in increasing chances of convection next week. \nExpect rain chances to really ramp up just beyond the extended \nforecast, when the west coast trough makes landfall and begins \ntilting negative.",
